// What is NOT wrong with this file: a block local does not die with its
// `end`. §5.3.2 is explicit the other way — "all identifiers declared within a
// named sequential block can be accessed outside the scope in which they are
// declared" — and it prints `moduleVar = myscope.localVar;` as legal. So
// `inner.hidden` on line 26 would compile. The declaration outlives the block;
// only the SPELLING used below cannot reach it.
//
// What is wrong is the bare name. §6.8: "if an identifier is referenced
// directly (without a hierarchical path) ... the search continues upward until
// ... a module boundary" — upward only, never down into a nested named block.
// `hidden` unqualified therefore resolves against nothing. The rejection is
// about the missing qualifier, not about the lifetime of the declaration, and
// §5.3.2's positive form is the fixture this one does not replace.
//
// named_block_locals.va tests that a block-local is usable inside its block,
// and ch06_hierarchy/local_scope_shadow.va tests that the enclosing scope stays
// visible from inside; both are permissive directions.
//
// (VerA answers with E0314 "unknown identifier", not E0313 "unknown variable",
// even though E0313's explain text advertises this exact case. Pinned against
// the code that actually fires; E0313 remains cited by zero fixtures.)
//! lrm 6.8
//! lrm 5.3.2
//! reject E0314
module ch5_named_block_scope(p);
  inout p; electrical p;
  real out;
  analog begin
    begin : inner
      real hidden;
      hidden = V(p);
    end
    out = hidden;            // illegal: unqualified; §5.3.2 spells it inner.hidden
    I(p) <+ out;
  end
endmodule
